e tile we obtain a tiling that uses exactly the numbers of tiles given by the right‑hand sides of the inequalities.  Hence for a given permutation the minimum possible number of tiles is\n   \\[\n   \\tau_{\\min}(p)=\\bigl(n-1\\bigr)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Optimising over all permutations.**  \n   The term \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|a_i-a_{i-1}|\\) is the total variation of the sequence \\((a_i)\\).  Since \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), any such sequence must travel from the minimum \\(0\\) to the maximum \\(n-1\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=2}^{n}|a_i-a_{i-1}|\\;\\ge\\;n-1,\n   \\]\n   with equality precisely when \\((a_i)\\) is monotone (either increasing or decreasing).  Substituting the minimum value into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   \\tau_{\\min}= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (uncovered squares on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a vertical tile covering rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(j\\) (size \\((n-j)\\times1\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place a vertical tile covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,j-1\\) in column \\(j\\) (size \\((j-1)\\times1\\)).  \n   These are \\(n-1\\) tiles on the left of the diagonal and \\(n-1\\) tiles on the right, altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and they cover every cell except the diagonal squares.\n\n6. **Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   Plugging \\(n=2025\\) into \\(2n-2\\) yields\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\nFinally, the configuration with “one = 0’’ (i.e. zero uncovered squares per row/column, or zero tiles) violates the requirement that each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered unit square; therefore it does **not** satisfy the constraints.  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered square be \\((i,p(i))\\), where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\na_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le a_i\\le n-1),\\qquad \nb_i:=n-p(i)=n-1-a_i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a_i+b_i=n-1\\) for every \\(i\\).  The set of covered unit squares is the complement of the permutation matrix \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\}\\) and splits naturally into\n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\qquad\nR:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA *tile* is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists entirely of covered squares; tiles may not overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for a fixed permutation\n\nConsider the left region \\(L\\).  For a fixed column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c:=\\{\\,i\\mid a_i\\ge c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_c\\) is a union of disjoint intervals of rows; each interval is a *vertical block* of cells in column \\(c\\) that must be covered.  Any tile intersecting column \\(c\\) is a rectangle; its intersection with column \\(c\\) is a single vertical interval, therefore a tile can cover **at most one** block of \\(S_c\\).  Consequently the number of tiles intersecting column \\(c\\) is at least the number of blocks of \\(S_c\\).\n\nLet \\(B_c\\) denote the number of blocks of \\(S_c\\).  When we increase the column index from \\(c-1\\) to \\(c\\), a new block appears precisely when a row \\(i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\na_i\\ge c\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a_{i-1}<c,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere we set \\(a_0:=0\\) (the “row 0’’ is taken to have \\(a_0=0\\) so that (2) also counts the first block in column 1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nB_c=\\bigl|\\{\\,i\\mid a_i\\ge c\\text{ and }a_{i-1}<c\\,\\}\\bigr|\n   =\\max\\bigl(0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nsummed over all \\(i\\).  Summing over all columns gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}B_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr),\n   \\qquad a_0:=0. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince each tile contributes to at most one block in each column, the total number \\(\\tau_L\\) of tiles covering \\(L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\tau_L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}B_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) (now the relevant sequence is \\(a_i\\) but the “initial’’ value is \\(a_0:=n-1\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\tau_R\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,a_{i-1}-a_i\\bigr),\n   \\qquad a_0:=n-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding (4) and (5) and observing that for \\(i=1\\) the two terms give \\(a_1+(n-1-a_1)=n-1\\), we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\tau_L+\\tau_R\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The bound is attainable\n\nFor any fixed permutation we can achieve equality in (4) and (5) as follows.\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) and each block of \\(S_c\\) place a vertical tile of width 1 that exactly covers that block.  Different blocks are disjoint, so the tiles are disjoint.  The number of tiles produced is precisely \\(\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}B_c\\), i.e. the right‑hand side of (4).\n\n*Right region.*  \nApply the to the columns of \\(R\\) (which correspond to the values \\(b_i=n-1-a_i\\)).  This yields exactly the number of tiles given by (5).\n\nThus for the permutation \\(p\\) the minimum possible number of tiles equals the right‑hand side of (6):\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\min}(p)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Optimising over all permutations\n\nThe sequence \\((a_1,\\dots ,a_n)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Its *total variation*  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(V\\ge n-1\\).  Indeed, the values \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\) must both appear, and travelling from the smallest to the largest value along the sequence requires a total distance of at least \\(n-1\\); any back‑and‑forth only increases the sum.  Equality \\(V=n-1\\) holds exactly when the sequence is monotone, i.e. either  \n\n\\[\na_1<a_2<\\dots <a_n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a_1>a_2>\\dots >a_n .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the minimal possible value of \\(V\\) into (7) gives the global minimum\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\min}= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit construction attaining the global minimum\n\nTake the *identity* permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); then \\(a_i=i-1\\) is strictly increasing.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n- **Left of the diagonal.**  \n  For each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a tile of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(c\\).  These are \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.\n\n- **Right of the diagonal.**  \n  For each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place a tile of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) in column \\(c\\).  Again we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.\n\nAll cells except the diagonal are covered, the tiles are pairwise disjoint, and the total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer to the explicit sub‑question\n\nThe suggestion “one = 0’’ would mean that a row (or a column) contains **zero** uncovered squares.  The problem statement requires **exactly one** uncovered square in every row and every column, so a configuration with zero uncovered squares (or equivalently, with zero tiles) violates the constraints and is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that 