1}\\bigr),\n   \\qquad a_0:=0. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince each tile contributes to at most one block in each column, the total number \\(\\tau_L\\) of tiles covering \\(L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\tau_L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}B_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) (now the relevant sequence is \\(a_i\\) but the “initial’’ value is \\(a_0:=n-1\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\tau_R\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,a_{i-1}-a_i\\bigr),\n   \\qquad a_0:=n-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding (4) and (5) and observing that for \\(i=1\\) the two terms give \\(a_1+(n-1-a_1)=n-1\\), we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\tau_L+\\tau_R\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The bound is attainable\n\nFor any fixed permutation we can achieve equality in (4) and (5) as follows.\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) and each block of \\(S_c\\) place a vertical tile of width 1 that exactly covers that block.  Different blocks are disjoint, so the tiles are disjoint.  The number of tiles produced is precisely \\(\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}B_c\\), i.e. the right‑hand side of (4).\n\n*Right region.*  \nApply the to the columns of \\(R\\) (which correspond to the values \\(b_i=n-1-a_i\\)).  This yields exactly the number of tiles given by (5).\n\nThus for the permutation \\(p\\) the minimum possible number of tiles equals the right‑hand side of (6):\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\min}(p)= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Optimising over all permutations\n\nThe sequence \\((a_1,\\dots ,a_n)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Its *total variation*  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(V\\ge n-1\\).  Indeed, the values \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\) must both appear, and travelling from the smallest to the largest value along the sequence requires a total distance of at least \\(n-1\\); any back‑and‑forth only increases the sum.  Equality \\(V=n-1\\) holds exactly when the sequence is monotone, i.e. either  \n\n\\[\na_1<a_2<\\dots <a_n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a_1>a_2>\\dots >a_n .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the minimal possible value of \\(V\\) into (7) gives the global minimum\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\min}= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit construction attaining the global minimum\n\nTake the *identity* permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); then \\(a_i=i-1\\) is strictly increasing.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n- **Left of the diagonal.**  \n  For each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a tile of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(c\\).  These are \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.\n\n- **Right of the diagonal.**  \n  For each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place a tile of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\) covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) in column \\(c\\).  Again we obtain \\(n-1\\) disjoint vertical tiles.\n\nAll cells except the diagonal are covered, the tiles are pairwise disjoint, and the total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer to the explicit sub‑question\n\nThe suggestion “one = 0’’ would mean that a row (or a column) contains **zero** uncovered squares.  The problem statement requires **exactly one** uncovered square in every row and every column, so a configuration with zero uncovered squares (or equivalently, with zero tiles) violates the constraints and is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that Mati