 explicit tiling for \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) is:\n   - **Lower triangle** \\(\\{(i,j):j<i\\}\\): for each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)) place a vertical rectangle covering rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\) in that column.\n   - **Upper triangle** \\(\\{(i,j):j>i\\}\\): for each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) place a horizontal rectangle covering columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) in that row.\n\n   This uses \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles, exactly the lower bound.\n\n5. **Small cases (illustration).**  \n   - \\(n=1\\): the single square is uncovered; \\(0\\) tiles are needed.  \n   - \\(n=2\\): uncovered squares \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); the other two squares are covered by two \\(1\\times1\\) tiles, giving \\(2=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   - \\(n=3\\): uncovered squares \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\); the lower triangle is tiled by a \\(2\\times1\\) vertical tile and a \\(1\\times1\\) tile, the upper triangle by a \\(1\\times2\\) horizontal tile and a \\(1\\times1\\) tile – total \\(4=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n   The same pattern scales to any \\(n\\).\n\nThus the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Denote the rows and columns of the board by \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Decomposition of the covered region  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\; \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of the squares strictly to the left of the uncovered square in each row; \\(R\\) consists of those strictly to the right.  The two sets are disjoint and together contain all covered squares.\n\nA rectangle used as a tile cannot contain any uncovered square, hence it must lie entirely inside \\(L\\) or inside \\(R\\).  Consequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\n               +\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Number of rectangles needed for \\(L\\)\n\nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_j=\\{\\,i\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\,\\}= \\{\\,i\\mid (i,j)\\in L\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll squares of column \\(j\\) that belong to \\(L\\) are precisely the rows in \\(S_j\\).  \nA rectangle covering part of column \\(j\\) must consist of a *consecutive* block of rows belonging to \\(S_j\\); thus each maximal consecutive block of rows in \\(S_j\\) yields one rectangle.  Hence the number of rectangles that cover column \\(j\\) equals the number of connected components (intervals) of the set \\(S_j\\).\n\nLet us count these intervals by counting their starting rows.  \nFor a row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) and a column \\(j\\) we have a start of a new interval in column \\(j\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\ni\\in S_j\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i=1\\text{ or }i-1\\notin S_j).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i\\in S_j\\) means \\(\\pi(i)>j\\) and \\(i-1\\notin S_j\\) means \\(\\pi(i-1)\\le j\\) (with the convention \\(\\pi(0)=0\\)), condition (4) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i-1)\\le j<\\pi(i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1\\) the lower bound \\(\\pi(0)=0\\) gives \\(0\\le j<\\pi(1)\\); therefore the number of columns \\(j\\) that start an interval at row \\(1\\) equals \\(\\pi(1)-1\\).\n\nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the integers \\(j\\) satisfying (5) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nj=\\pi(i-1),\\;\\pi(i-1)+1,\\dots ,\\pi(i)-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich exist only when \\(\\pi(i)>\\pi(i-1)\\).  Their count is \\(\\max\\{0,\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)\\}\\).\n\nSumming over all rows we obtain the total number of intervals in all columns, i.e. the number of rectangles covering \\(L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\n   =(\\pi(1)-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Number of rectangles needed for \\(R\\)\n\nThe analysis for \\(R\\) is symmetric.  Define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)\\le n-j\\,\\}= \\{\\,i\\mid (i,n-j)\\in R\\,\\},\n\\qquad 1\\le j\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA start of a new interval in column \\(j\\) of \\(R\\) occurs when  \n\n\\[\ni\\in T_j\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i=1\\text{ or }i-1\\notin T_j).\n\\]\n\nRepeating the previous argument (now with the inequality \\(\\pi(i)\\le n-j\\)) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R\n   =(n-\\pi(1))+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(0,\\pi(i-1)-\\pi(i)\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Total number of tiles for a given permutation  \n\nAdding (6) and (7) and using \\(\\max\\{0,a\\}+\\max\\{0,-a\\}=|a|\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\text{tiles}\n   &=\\bigl(\\pi(1)-1\\bigr)+(n-\\pi(1))\n     +\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\Bigl(\\max\\bigl(0,\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)\\bigr)\n                         +\\max\\bigl(0,\\pi(i-1)-\\pi(i)\\bigr)\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n   &= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)\\bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus for any permutation \\(\\pi\\) the number of tiles required is exactly the expression on the right‑hand side of (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   A universal lower bound  \n\nConsider the integers \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  In the sequence \\(\\pi(1),\\pi(2),\\dots ,\\pi(n)\\) there must be at least one index \\(i\\) for which \\(\\pi(i-1)\\le k<\\pi(i)\\) or \\(\\pi(i-1)>k\\ge\\pi(i)\\); otherwise all entries would lie either \\(\\le k\\) or \\(>k\\), contradicting that the permutation contains both kinds of numbers.  Hence each \\(k\\) contributes at least \\(1\\) to the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)\\bigr|\\ge n-1,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand from (8)\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Achieving the bound  \n\nTake the monotone permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the identity).  Then \\(|\\pi(i)-\\pi(i-1)|=1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\), so the sum in (8) equals \\(n-1\\) and (10) becomes an equality:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n**Explicit tiling for \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).**  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Lower triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) rectangles, each of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n*Upper triangle.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle covering  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain we obtain \\(n-1\\) rectangles, each of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).\n\nAll squares of the board except the diagonal are covered exactly once, and the total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   Application to the original problem  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.   Small‑size illustrations (the “one = 1’’ case)  \n\n- **\\(n=1\\).** The single square must be the uncovered one; no tile is needed (0 = \\(2\\cdot1-2\\)).  \n- **\\(n=2\\).** Uncovered squares \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); the remaining two squares are covered by two \\(1\\times1\\) tiles (2 = \\(2\\cdot2-2\\)).  \n- **\\(n=3\\).** Uncovered squares \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\); the lower triangle is tiled by a \\(2\\times1\\) vertical tile and a \\(1\\times1\\) tile, the upper triangle by a \\(1\\times2\\) horizontal tile and a \\(1\\times1\\) tile – total 4 tiles (4 = \\(2\\cdot3-2\\)).  \n\nThese examples follow exactly the construction described in §6 and confirm the formula for all \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid, while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
