a rectangle form a contiguous interval, they cannot intersect two distinct runs of \\(S_j\\). \u220e  \n\n   Consequently, for each column \\(j\\) at least \\(r_j\\) left\u2011side tiles have rightmost column \\(j\\).  Summing over all columns gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{left\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} r_j. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma 2.* For every \\(j\\) we have \\(r_j\\ge 1\\).  \n   *Proof.* The row \\(i_0\\) with \\(\\pi(i_0)=n\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(i_0)>j\\) for all \\(j<n\\); hence \\(i_0\\in S_j\\), so \\(S_j\\neq\\varnothing\\) and therefore possesses at least one run. \u220e  \n\n   From Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} r_j\\ge n-1\\).  Together with (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{left\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Right\u2011side tiles.**  \n   Reflect the board horizontally: the permutation \\(\\pi'(i)=n+1-\\pi(i)\\) describes the uncovered squares after reflection.  The left\u2011side tiles for \\(\\pi'\\) correspond exactly to the right\u2011side tiles for the original \\(\\pi\\).  Applying the bound (2) to \\(\\pi'\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{right\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Overall lower bound.**  \n   Adding (2) and (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2(n-1). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   *Left\u2011side tiles.* For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T_j^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  It covers precisely the left\u2011side squares in column \\(j\\).\n\n   *Right\u2011side tiles.* For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T_j^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  It covers precisely the right\u2011side squares in column \\(j\\).\n\n   The two families are disjoint because \\(T_j^{\\text{L}}\\) uses rows \\(>j\\) while \\(T_j^{\\text{R}}\\) uses rows \\(<j\\).  Hence every unit square except the diagonal is covered exactly once, and each row and column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  The total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (4) and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We work with the integer set \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there is a function  \n\\(\\pi:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\([n]\\).\n\n#### 2.  Every tile is entirely on one side of the uncovered squares  \n\nConsider a tile \\(T\\).  Its set of squares is a rectangle  \n\\([c,d]\\times[a,b]\\) with integer coordinates \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\).  \nSuppose that \\(T\\) covers rows \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\) with \\(\\pi(i)\\le d\\) and \\(\\pi(i')\\ge c\\).  \nBecause \\(c\\le d\\), the interval \\([c,d]\\) contains an integer \\(k\\).  \nSince \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, there exists a row \\(j\\) with \\(\\pi(j)=k\\).  \nThe rows covered by \\(T\\) form a contiguous interval; thus \\(j\\) lies between \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\) and is also covered by \\(T\\).  \nConsequently \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) is a square of \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered.  \nHence for all rows \\(i\\) covered by \\(T\\) either \\(d<\\pi(i)\\) (the tile lies completely to the left of the uncovered square) or \\(c>\\pi(i)\\) (the tile lies completely to the right).  \nBecause the same inequality must hold for every covered row, a tile is of **one** of the two types:\n\n* **left\u2011side tile:** \\(d<\\pi(i)\\) for all covered rows \\(i\\);\n* **right\u2011side tile:** \\(c>\\pi(i)\\) for all covered rows \\(i\\).\n\nThus the collection of tiles splits into left\u2011side and right\u2011side tiles, and no tile belongs to both families.\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for left\u2011side tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j=\\{\\,i\\in[n]\\mid \\pi(i)>j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe squares that must be covered in column \\(j\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in S_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(S_j\\) as a disjoint union of maximal intervals of consecutive integers; denote their number by \\(r_j\\) (the **run number** of \\(S_j\\)).  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(T\\) be a left\u2011side tile whose rightmost column equals \\(j\\) (i.e. \\(d=j\\)).  Then the set of rows covered by \\(T\\) is contained in a single run of \\(S_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(T\\) contains column \\(j\\), for any row \\(i\\) covered by \\(T\\) we have \\(j\\le d<\\pi(i)\\); thus \\(i\\in S_j\\).  The rows of a rectangle form a contiguous interval, so they cannot intersect two distinct runs of \\(S_j\\) (which are separated by rows not belonging to \\(S_j\\)). \u220e  \n\nConsequently, for a fixed column \\(j\\) each run of \\(S_j\\) requires a distinct left\u2011side tile whose rightmost column is \\(j\\).  Hence at least \\(r_j\\) left\u2011side tiles have rightmost column \\(j\\).  Summing over all columns gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{left\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} r_j. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nFor every \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have \\(r_j\\ge1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(i_0\\) be the unique row with \\(\\pi(i_0)=n\\).  Since \\(n>j\\), we have \\(i_0\\in S_j\\); thus \\(S_j\\neq\\varnothing\\).  Any non\u2011empty set of integers possesses at least one maximal interval of consecutive integers, i.e. at least one run. \u220e  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2, \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} r_j\\ge n-1\\).  Together with (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{left\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for right\u2011side tiles  \n\nReflect the board horizontally.  The reflected uncovered squares correspond to the permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi'(i)=n+1-\\pi(i),\\qquad i\\in[n].\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011side tiles for \\(\\pi'\\) are exactly the right\u2011side tiles for the original \\(\\pi\\).  Applying (2) to \\(\\pi'\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{right\u2011side tiles}\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.  Overall lower bound  \n\nAdding (2) and (3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this lower bound equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left\u2011side tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has dimensions \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  It covers exactly the left\u2011side squares in column \\(j\\).\n\n*Right\u2011side tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nof dimensions \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  It covers exactly the right\u2011side squares in column \\(j\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint because in column \\(j\\) the left\u2011side tile uses rows \\(>j\\) while the right\u2011side tile uses rows \\(<j\\).  Hence every unit square except the diagonal is covered exactly once, and each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (4) and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing the output, carefully review the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure:\n\n* All statements are rigorously justified and no logical gaps remain.\n* Every mathematical expression is enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n* The final answer is presented exactly as `\\boxed{4048}`.\n* The required section headings and order (`### Summary ###`, `### Detailed Solution ###`, `### Self-Correction Instruction ###`) are present and contain no extraneous material."
