nIn particular the smallest column of \\(R\\) must satisfy \\(c_{1}>r_{2}\\); otherwise a cell with row index \\(c_{1}\\) and column index \\(c_{1}\\) (a diagonal cell) would belong to \\(R\\).  \nHence \\(c_{1}\\ge r_{2}+1\\).  \n\nIf \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) then \\(i\\in[r_{1},r_{2}]\\) and \\(i+1\\in[c_{1},c_{2}]\\).  \nFrom \\(c_{1}>r_{2}\\) we obtain \\(i+1>r_{2}\\); therefore \\(i=r_{2}\\).  \nThus any element of \\(S_{U}\\) lying in \\(R\\) must be the unique cell \\((r_{2},r_{2}+1)\\). \u220e\n\nSince \\(|S_{U}|=n-1\\) and each rectangle of \\(U\\) covers at most one element of \\(S_{U}\\), any tiling of \\(U\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the lower triangle  \n\nBy symmetry, applying Lemma\u202f2 to the set  \n\\[\nS_{L}=\\{(i,i-1):2\\le i\\le n\\}\\subseteq L\n\\]\nyields that each rectangle of \\(L\\) contains at most one element of \\(S_{L}\\).  \nSince \\(|S_{L}|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound  \n\nAny admissible tiling of the whole board must consist of a family \\(\\mathcal R_{U}\\) of rectangles covering \\(U\\) and a family \\(\\mathcal R_{L}\\) covering \\(L\\).  \nFrom Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R_{U}|\\ge n-1,\\qquad |\\mathcal R_{L}|\\ge n-1.\n\\]\nTherefore the total number of tiles satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R_{U}|+|\\mathcal R_{L}|\\ge 2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine the following \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Upper\u2011triangle tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let\n\\[\nR_{i}^{U}=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_{i}^{U}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle lying completely in \\(U\\).\n\n*Lower\u2011triangle tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) let\n\\[\nR_{i}^{L}=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(R_{i}^{L}\\) is a \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) rectangle lying completely in \\(L\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R_{i}^{U}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_{i}^{L}\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and their union equals\n\\[\nU\\cup L = B\\setminus D .\n\\]\nThus we have produced a tiling with exactly \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nInequality\u202f(1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section\u202f6 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Numerical answer  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
