e disjoint (different rows, and within a given row the column intervals are disjoint).  Their union equals  \n\n\\[\nU\\cup L = B\\setminus D,\n\\]  \n\nso every unit square except the diagonal ones is covered exactly once.  The total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality and numerical answer  \n\nInequality (2) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 6 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above. Verify that every statement contributes directly to the final, coherent mathematical argument."
