ve to be partitioned into axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) that contain no\npoint of \\(U\\).  A rectangle is a product \\(I\\times J\\) where\n\\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of\nconsecutive rows and columns, respectively, and  \n\n\\[\n(I\\times J)\\cap U=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A lower bound  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1   Left and right rectangles  \n\nFor a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) and a row \\(i\\in I\\) we have either\n\\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\) (the rectangle lies **left** of the hole\nin that row) or\n\\(J\\subseteq\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the rectangle lies **right** of the hole).\nIf a rectangle contains two rows whose holes lie on opposite sides of \\(J\\),\nthe rectangle would contain a hole, which is forbidden.  Consequently every\nrectangle is *entirely* left of the holes of all its rows, or *entirely* right of\nthem.  We call the former **left rectangles** and the latter **right\nrectangles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2   Distinguishing rows  \n\nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal R\\) of all rectangles in a tiling.\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the binary vector  \n\n\\[\nv(i)=\\bigl(\\mathbf 1_{R\\in\\mathcal R}(i\\in I_R)\\bigr)_{R\\in\\mathcal R},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the \\(R\\)-th coordinate of \\(v(i)\\) is \\(1\\) iff row \\(i\\) belongs to the\nrow‑set of rectangle \\(R\\).  \n\nLet \\(i<j\\) and assume \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(j)\\).\nAll columns \\(c\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<c<\\sigma(j)\\) are to the **right** of the hole\nin row \\(i\\) and to the **left** of the hole in row \\(j\\).  Therefore the cells\n\\((i,c)\\) must be covered by a right rectangle, while the cells \\((j,c)\\) must be\ncovered by a left rectangle.  No rectangle can be simultaneously left and right,\nhence there