 unit squares, where \\((r,c)\\) denotes the square in row \\(r\\) and column \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThus the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nDefine the set  \n\\[\nS=\n\\underbrace{\\{(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}}_{S_{1}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}}_{S_{2}}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nEvery element of \\(S\\) is a non‑hole square, because for each \\(i\\) the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) (resp. \\(\\sigma(i)\\)) differs from the hole column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (resp. \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\)).  \nClearly \\(|S|=2n-2\\).\n\nWe claim that **no axis‑aligned rectangle that avoids all holes can contain two distinct points of \\(S\\).**  \nLet \\(x=(r_{1},c_{1})\\) and \\(y=(r_{2},c_{2})\\) be distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nThree possibilities occur.\n\n*Case A – both points belong to \\(S_{1}\\).*  \nThen \\(x=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\(y=(j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThe rectangle \\(R\\) spanned by \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is  \n\\[\nR=[i,j]\\times[\\min\\{\\sigma(i+1),\\sigma(j+1)\\},\\max\\{\\sigma(i+1),\\sigma(j+1)\\}].\n\\]\nSince the row \\(i+1\\) lies in \\([i,j]\\) and the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) is one endpoint of the column interval, the square \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in H\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  \nHence \\(R\\) contains a hole.\n\n*Case B – both points belong to \\(S_{2}\\).*  \nThen \\(x=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\(y=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThe rectangle spanned by them is  \n\\[\nR=[i+1,j+1]\\times[\\min\\{\\sigma(i),\\sigma(j)\\},\\max\\{\\sigma(i),\\sigma(j)\\}].\n\\]\nIf \\(j=i+1\\) the column interval has endpoint \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\); the square \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in H\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \nIf \\(j\\ge i+2\\), among the \\(j-i+1\\ge3\\) distinct numbers\n\\(\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i+1),\\dots ,\\sigma(j)\\) the two extremes are \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\); therefore at least one intermediate value, say \\(\\sigma(k)\\) with \\(i<k<j\\), lies strictly between them.  \nConsequently the square \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\in H\\) is inside \\(R\\).  \nThus \\(R\\) always contains a hole.\n\n*Case C – one point from \\(S_{1}\\) and one from \\(S_{2}\\).*  \nAssume \\(x=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\in S_{1}\\) and \\(y=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\in S_{2}\\) with \\(i\\le j\\).  \nThe rectangle spanned by them is  \n\\[\nR=[i,j+1]\\times[\\min\\{\\sigma(i+1),\\sigma(j)\\},\\max\\{\\sigma(i+1),\\sigma(j)\\}].\n\\]\nThe row \\(i+1\\) belongs to \\([i,j+1]\\) and the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) is an endpoint of the column interval, so \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in H\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \nAgain the rectangle contains a hole.\n\nIn every case a rectangle covering two distinct points of \\(S\\) must contain a hole, which is forbidden.  \nHence each admissible rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling of the board minus the holes needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 2n-2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); thus the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) define two vertical rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_j^{\\text{up}}   &=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}, &&\\text{(empty when }j=1),\\\\[2pt]\nR_j^{\\text{down}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}, &&\\text{(empty when }j=n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery rectangle consists of whole unit squares, its sides lie on grid lines, and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.  \nTogether they cover all squares of the board except the diagonal holes, because in column \\(j\\) the only uncovered square is \\((j,j)\\).  \n\nThe number of non‑empty rectangles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction (4) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above."
