.*  \nLet \\(D=\\{i_{1}<i_{2}<\\dots <i_{L}\\}\\) be a decreasing subsequence of length \\(L=L(\\pi)\\) (so \\(\\pi(i_{1})>\\pi(i_{2})>\\dots >\\pi(i_{L})\\)).  \nFor each \\(i_{k}\\in D\\) define  \n\n\\[\ns_{i_{k}}=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i_{k},\\,\\pi(i_{k})-1), & \\text{if }\\pi(i_{k})>1,\\\\[2mm]\n(i_{k},\\,\\pi(i_{k})+1), & \\text{if }\\pi(i_{k})=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus every \\(s_{i_{k}}\\) is a unit square that is **not** a hole.  \n\nSimilarly, let \\(I=\\{j_{1}<j_{2}<\\dots <j_{M}\\}\\) be an increasing subsequence of length \\(M=M(\\pi)\\) (so \\(\\pi(j_{1})<\\pi(j_{2})<\\dots <\\pi(j_{M})\\)).  \nFor each \\(j_{k}\\in I\\) define  \n\n\\[\nt_{j_{k}}=\n\\begin{cases}\n(j_{k},\\,\\pi(j_{k})+1), & \\text{if }\\pi(j_{k})<n,\\\\[2mm]\n(j_{k},\\,\\pi(j_{k})-1), & \\text{if }\\pi(j_{k})=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAgain each \\(t_{j_{k}}\\) is a non‑hole square.  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}= \\{s_{i_{k}}\\mid 1\\le k\\le L\\}\\cup\\{t_{j_{k}}\\mid 1\\le k\\le M\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(L+M\\) squares (the two subsequences can be chosen to be disjoint; if they intersect we simply omit one of the two squares belonging to the common row, which does not affect the argument).  \n\nWe claim that **no axis‑parallel rectangle avoiding all holes can contain two distinct squares of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\)**.  \nIndeed, consider two distinct squares of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n*Both from the decreasing family.*  \nThey have the form \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\((i',\\pi(i')-1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\) and \\(\\pi(i)>\\pi(i')\\).  \nAny rectangle containing them must contain the whole row interval \\([i,i']\\) and the column interval \\([\\pi(i')-1,\\pi(i)-1]\\).  \nSince \\(\\pi(i')\\) lies between the two column extremes, the square \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) (which is a hole) belongs to the rectangle – a contradiction.\n\n*Both from the increasing family.*  \nThey are \\((j,\\pi(j)+1)\\) and \\((j',\\pi(j')+1)\\) with \\(j<j'\\) and \\(\\pi(j)<\\pi(j')\\).  \nA rectangle covering them contains the column interval \\([\\pi(j)+1,\\pi(j')+1]\\) and the row interval \\([j,j']\\); the hole \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) lies inside, again a contradiction.\n\n*One from each family.*  \nAssume the decreasing square is \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and the increasing one is \\((j,\\pi(j)+1)\\).  \nIf \\(i=j\\) the two squares are distinct only when \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) or \\(\\pi(i)=n\\); in either case the rectangle would contain the unique hole of that row.  \nIf \\(i<j\\) then \\(\\pi(i)>\\pi(j)\\) (otherwise the two squares would be comparable and belong to the same monotone subsequence, which is impossible).  \nThe rectangle’s column interval is \\([\\pi(j)+1,\\pi(i)-1]\\); it contains the column \\(\\pi(j)\\), so the hole \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) lies inside.  \nThe case \\(j<i\\) is symmetric.  \n\nThus any rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  \nSince a tiling is a partition, each rectangle contributes at most one element of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\); consequently at least \\(|\\mathcal{F}|=L+M\\) rectangles are necessary. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Erdős–Szekeres)\n\nFor every permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) one has  \n\\[\nL(\\pi)\\,M(\\pi)\\ge n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nConsequently  \n\\[\nL(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\ge 2\\bigl\\lceil\\sqrt{n}\\,\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe classical Erdős–Szekeres theorem states that any sequence of \\(n\\) distinct numbers contains either an increasing subsequence of length \\(\\lceil\\sqrt{n}\\,\\rceil\\) or a decreasing subsequence of that length.  \nEquivalently, for every permutation the product of the lengths of a longest increasing and a longest decreasing subsequence is at least \\(n\\).  \nInequality (4) follows from the elementary bound \\(x+y\\ge 2\\sqrt{xy}\\) applied to \\(x=L(\\pi)\\), \\(y=M(\\pi)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (upper bound)\n\nFor any permutation \\(\\pi\\) the complement of its permutation matrix can be tiled with exactly \\(L(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Left part.**  \n   By Dilworth’s theorem the set of points \\(\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\}\\) ordered by the product order \\((i,j)\\le (i',j')\\iff i\\le i',\\,j\\le j'\\) can be partitioned into \\(L(\\pi)\\) *chains*, i.e. into \\(L(\\pi)\\) increasing subsequences  \n   \\[\n   I_{1},I_{2},\\dots ,I_{L(\\pi)} .\n   \\]\n   For a chain \\(I\\) let  \n   \\[\n   c(I)=\\min_{i\\in I}\\pi(i)-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   (If the minimum equals \\(1\\) we set \\(c(I)=0\\) and the corresponding rectangle is empty.)  \n   Define the rectangle  \n   \\[\n   R_{I}=I\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,c(I)\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Because every row \\(i\\in I\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(i)>c(I)\\), all squares of \\(R_{I}\\) lie strictly left of the hole in that row; hence \\(R_{I}\\) is a legal tile.  \n   The rectangles \\(R_{I_{1}},\\dots ,R_{I_{L(\\pi)}}\\) are pairwise disjoint because the chains are disjoint in rows.\n\n2. **Right part.**  \n   Dually, the points can be partitioned into \\(M(\\pi)\\) *antichains* of the reverse order, i.e. into \\(M(\\pi)\\) decreasing subsequences  \n   \\[\n   D_{1},D_{2},\\dots ,D_{M(\\pi)} .\n   \\]\n   For a decreasing subsequence \\(D\\) let  \n   \\[\n   d(D)=\\max_{i\\in D}\\pi(i)+1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   (If the maximum equals \\(n\\) we set \\(d(D)=n+1\\) and the rectangle is empty.)  \n   Define the rectangle  \n   \\[\n   S_{D}=D\\times\\{d(D),d(D)+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   For every \\(i\\in D\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)<d(D)\\); therefore all squares of \\(S_{D}\\) lie strictly to the right of the hole in that row, so \\(S_{D}\\) is legal.  \n   The rectangles \\(S_{D_{1}},\\dots ,S_{D_{M(\\pi)}}\\) are disjoint for the same reason as above.\n\n3. **Disjointness of the two families.**  \n   Any rectangle \\(R_{I}\\) uses only columns \\(\\le c(I)\\), while any rectangle \\(S_{D}\\) uses only columns \\(\\ge d(D)\\).  \n   By construction \\(c(I)<\\pi(i)\\le d(D)\\) for every row \\(i\\) belonging to \\(I\\) or \\(D\\); hence the column intervals of a left rectangle and a right rectangle are disjoint, and consequently the two families are mutually disjoint.\n\n4. **Coverage.**  \n   Let \\((i,j)\\) be a square not containing a hole.  \n   If \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) then \\(j\\le c(I)\\) for the unique increasing chain \\(I\\) that contains row \\(i\\); thus \\((i,j)\\in R_{I}\\).  \n   If \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) then \\(j\\ge d(D)\\) for the unique decreasing chain \\(D\\) that contains row \\(i\\); thus \\((i,j)\\in S_{D}\\).  \n   Hence every non‑hole square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.\n\nThus we have produced a tiling with precisely \\(L(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the global minimum  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 any tiling needs at least \\(L(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\) rectangles, and Lemma 3 shows that this number is always attainable.  \nConsequently the minimal possible number of tiles for a given permutation equals \\(L(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\).  \nLemma 2 gives the universal lower bound\n\\[\nL(\\pi)+M(\\pi)\\ge 2\\bigl\\lceil\\sqrt{n}\\,\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe bound (9) is sharp.  \nAssume \\(n=k^{2}\\) (the case of a non‑square \\(n\\) is handled analogously).  \nConsider the permutation that consists of \\(k\\) decreasing blocks of length \\(k\\):\n\\[\n\\pi\\bigl(kb+r\\bigr)=k(b+1)-r+1\\qquad\n(b=0,\\dots ,k-1,\\;r=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nIn this permutation the longest decreasing subsequence has length \\(k\\) (any block) and the longest increasing subsequence also has length \\(k\\) (take the first element of each block).  \nHence \\(L(\\pi)=M(\\pi)=k\\) and the construction of Lemma 3 yields exactly \\(2k\\) rectangles.\n\nFor the problem at hand \\(n=2025=45^{2}\\); therefore \\(k=45\\) and the optimal value is  \n\n\\[\n2k=2\\cdot45=90 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Explicit tiling with 90 tiles  \n\nLet the rows be numbered \\(1,\\dots ,2025\\).  \nFor \\(b=0,1,\\dots ,44\\) define the *block*  \n\n\\[\nB_{b}=\\{45b+1,45b+2,\\dots ,45b+45\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn block \\(B_{b}\\) the holes are placed at the columns  \n\n\\[\n45(b+1),\\;45(b+1)-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;45b+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Left rectangle of block \\(b\\):*  \n\n\\[\nL_{b}=B_{b}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,45b\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor any row \\(i\\in B_{b}\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)\\ge 45b+1\\); therefore every column of \\(L_{b}\\) is strictly smaller than the hole column of that row, so \\(L_{b}\\) is a legal tile.\n\n*Right rectangle of block \\(b\\):*  \n\n\\[\nR_{b}=B_{b}\\times\\{45(b+1)+1,45(b+1)+2,\\dots ,2025\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHere \\(\\pi(i)\\le 45(b+1)\\) for all \\(i\\in B_{b}\\); consequently all columns of \\(R_{b}\\) lie strictly to the right of the hole in that row, and \\(R_{b}\\) is also legal.\n\nThe row intervals of different blocks are disjoint, and the column intervals of \\(L_{b}\\) and \\(R_{b}\\) are disjoint; hence the \\(90\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_{b},R_{b}\\mid b=0,\\dots ,44\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) belongs to the left rectangle of the block containing row \\(i\\); every square with \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) belongs to the right rectangle of that block.  \nThe holes themselves are not covered.  \nThus the board is tiled with exactly \\(90\\) rectangles, meeting the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{90}.\n\\]"
