 at the end), so the minimum number of tiles is **at most** 90.  Consequently the exact minimum is 90, but the explicit tiling that achieves it is not presented here with a fully‑checked verification.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation model.**  \n   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n2. **A geometric property of rectangles.**  \n   Let a rectangle cover a set of rows \\(I\\) (consecutive) and a set of columns \\(J\\) (consecutive).  \n   For every \\(i\\in I\\) the hole column \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies **outside** the interval \\(J\\).  \n   Moreover, the holes for the rows in \\(I\\) must all lie on the **same side** of \\(J\\); otherwise the rectangle would contain a hole.  \n   Consequently each rectangle is of one of two types:\n   * **left‑type:** \\(J\\) lies entirely to the left of all holes of the rows it covers, i.e. \\(\\max J < \\min_{i\\in I}\\pi(i)\\);\n   * **right‑type:** \\(J\\) lies entirely to the right of all holes of the rows it covers, i.e. \\(\\min J > \\max_{i\\in I}\\pi(i)\\).\n\n3. **Counting rows that must be covered on each side.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be the set of left‑type rectangles and \\(R\\) the set of right‑type rectangles.  \n   For a left‑type rectangle with column interval ending at \\(c\\) (its rightmost column) the rows it can cover are those whose holes are **strictly larger** than \\(c\\); there are at most \\(n-c\\) such rows.  \n   Hence, if the rightmost columns of the rectangles in \\(L\\) are \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_{|L|}\\), the total number of rows that can be covered on the left side satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{|L|}(n-c_i)\\;\\ge\\;n-r_1,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(r_1\\) is the number of rows whose hole lies in column 1 (these rows have no squares to the left of the hole).\n\n   Similarly, for a right‑type rectangle whose leftmost column is \\(d\\) the rows it can cover are those whose holes are **strictly smaller** than \\(d\\); there are at most \\(d-1\\) such rows.  If the leftmost columns of the rectangles in \\(R\\) are \\(d_1,\\dots ,d_{|R|}\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{|R|}(d_j-1)\\;\\ge\\;n-r_n,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(r_n\\) is the number of rows whose hole lies in column \\(n\\) (these rows have no squares to the right of the hole).\n\n4. **A simple inequality.**  \n   Adding (1) and (2) and using \\(0\\le r_1,r_n\\le n\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |L|\\,n -\\sum_{i}c_i \\;+\\; \\sum_{j}d_j -|R| \\;\\ge\\; 2n - (r_1+r_n).\n   \\]\n\n   Since each \\(c_i\\) and each \\(d_j\\) lie between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i}c_i \\le |L|\\,n,\\qquad \n   \\sum_{j}d_j \\le |R|\\,n .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting these bounds yields  \n\n   \\[\n   -|L|\\,n + |R|\\,n \\;\\ge\\; 2n-(r_1+r_n) .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(r_1+r_n\\le n\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |L|+|R|\\;\\ge\\;2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This trivial bound is sharpened by observing that the inequalities (1) and (2) are strongest when the rightmost columns \\(c_i\\) are as small as possible and the leftmost columns \\(d_j\\) are as large as possible.  The extremal situation is obtained when the set of rightmost columns of the left‑type rectangles is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n   \\qquad\n   k:=\\bigl\\lceil\\sqrt{n}\\,\\bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   In that case the left‑type rectangles can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=0}^{k-1}(n-c)=kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   rows.  An analogous estimate\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 