e board needs at least  \n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n     \\]  \n     tiles.\n\n4. **Upper bound – a construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place a tile covering the left part of the row:\n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n     \\]  \n     This gives \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place a tile covering the right part of the row:\n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n     \\]  \n     This gives another \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n\n   The two families of tiles are disjoint (they lie strictly left or strictly right of the diagonal) and together cover every square except the diagonal ones. Hence \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles must satisfy:\n\n* each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square,\n* each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square,\n* tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nDenote by \\((i,p(i))\\) the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map\n\\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two disjoint regions  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); a rectangle cannot\ncontain a point of \\(L\\) together with a point of \\(R\\), because any such\nrectangle would also contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) of the row it spans.\nConsequently each tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside\n\\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(|L|\\)  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ns_k=\\bigl(p^{-1}(k+1),\\,k\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe square \\(s_k\\) is the unit square immediately **left** of the uncovered\nsquare in column \\(k+1\\); hence \\(s_k\\in L\\).  \nThe rows \\(p^{-1}(k+1)\\) are distinct for different \\(k\\), and the columns\n\\(k\\) are distinct, so the squares \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_{n-1}\\) are pairwise\ndifferent.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{No rectangle can contain two distinct }s_j,s_k.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\(s_j\\) and \\(s_k\\) with \\(j<k\\).\nBecause the rectangle’s column interval is contiguous, it must contain every\ncolumn between \\(j\\) and \\(k\\), in particular column \\(j+1\\).\nColumn \\(j+1\\) is the column of the uncovered square \\((p^{-1}(j+1),j+1)\\);\nthe row \\(p^{-1}(j+1)\\) belongs to the rectangle (its column interval\ncontains \\(j+1\\) and its row interval contains the row of \\(s_j\\)).  \nThus the rectangle would cover the uncovered square \\((p^{-1}(j+1),j+1)\\),\ncontradicting the definition of a tile. ∎\n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one of the \\(n-1\\) squares\n\\(s_1,\\dots ,s_{n-1}\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover\nall of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for \\(|R|\\)  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nt_k=\\bigl(p^{-1}(k),\\,k+1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nthe square immediately **right** of the uncovered square in column \\(k\\);\nhence \\(t_k\\in R\\).  The squares \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_{n-1}\\) lie in distinct rows\nand columns.  If a rectangle contained both \\(t_j\\) and \\(t_k\\) with\n\\(j<k\\), its column interval would contain column \\(k\\), which is the hole\n\\((p^{-1}(k),k)\\) – a contradiction.  Therefore each rectangle covers at most\none of the \\(t_k\\), and at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nSince \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main\ndiagonal).  \n\n* For every row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  covering the part of the row left of the diagonal.  \n  These are \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n* For every row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  covering the part of the row right of the diagonal.  \n  These are another \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nThe two families are disjoint (the first family lies strictly left of the\ndiagonal, the second strictly right), they cover every square except the\n\\(n\\) diagonal squares, and each tile is a rectangle with sides on grid lines.\nHence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\ncoincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n------------------------------