vered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Define a set of “corner” squares.**  \n   For every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the square immediately to the left of the uncovered one,\n   \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\); for every row with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) consider the square immediately to the right,\n   \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\mid\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\mid\\sigma(i)<n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, \\(|C|=2n-2\\).\n\n3. **Lemma – a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(C\\).**  \n   Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) (axis‑parallel, with sides on grid lines) contains two distinct squares of \\(C\\).  \n   Three cases are possible:\n\n   *Both are left‑neighbors.*  \n   Let them be \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j)-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)\\) then the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) lies between the columns \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\); consequently \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) (an uncovered square) lies inside \\(R\\), a contradiction.  \n   The case \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(j)\\) is symmetric.  \n\n   *Both are right‑neighbors.*  \n   An identical argument (using the columns \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\)) yields a contradiction.\n\n   *One left‑neighbor and one right‑neighbor.*  \n   Assume the left‑neighbor is \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and the right‑neighbor is \\((j,\\sigma(j)+1)\\) with \\(i\\le j\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(j)\\) then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) lies between \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\), so \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is inside \\(R\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(j)\\) then column \\(\\sigma(j)\\) lies between \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\), so \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\) is inside \\(R\\).  \n   In either sub‑case an uncovered square is covered, contradicting the definition of a tile.\n\n   Hence any admissible rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(C\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound.**  \n   Because the \\(2n-2\\) squares of \\(C\\) must all be covered and each tile can cover at most one of them, any tiling needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \n   The uncovered squares are the diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   *Left side (columns left of the diagonal).*  \n   For each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, lie entirely left of the diagonal, and together cover every square with column \\(<\\) row.\n\n   *Right side (columns right of the diagonal).*  \n   For each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   H_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, lie entirely right of the diagonal, and together cover every square with column \\(>\\) row.\n\n   The two families \\(\\{V_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_r\\}\\) are disjoint, each tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, and the only uncovered squares are the diagonal ones.  The total number of tiles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime num