 Let's examine with a specific example: n=5, identity permutation. Suppose we take rows 2-4 (i=2,3,4) and columns 1-2 (L=1,R=2). For row 2, diagonal column is 2, which is included (since R=2). So rectangle includes uncovered square (2,2). Not allowed. If we take rows 2-4 and columns 3-5 (L=3,R=5). For row 4, diagonal column is 4, which is included. So not allowed. If we take rows 2-4 and columns 1-1 (L=R=1). For row 2, diagonal column is 2, not included. For row 3, diagonal column is 3, not included. For row 4, diagonal column is 4, not included. So rectangle includes only column 1 for rows 2-4. That's B side for all those rows (since column 1 < pi(i) for i>=2). So rectangle is entirely in B. Not crossing. To cross, we need rectangle includes both columns less than some pi(i) for some rows and columns greater than some pi(k) for other rows. That means column interval must contain both a column less than pi(i) and a column greater than pi(k). Since pi(i) = i, we need interval L <= something < i and R >= something > k. So interval must contain at least one column less than some row index and one column greater than some other row index. But the interval will contain all columns between L and R. If L <= i-1 and R >= k+1, then interval includes all columns from L to R. In particular, it includes column i (since i is between L and R if L <= i <= R). But we can choose i and k such that i < L (so L > i) and k > R (so R < k). But then interval does not contain column i or k. But then interval is entirely to the right of i and left of k. For crossing, we need L > i (so rectangle for row i includes only columns > i) and R < k (so rectangle for row k includes only columns < k). So interval must satisfy L > i and R < k. This is possible if i < L <= R < k. So interval is a subinterval of (i, k). For row i, all columns in interval are > i, so rectangle squares are in A for row i. For row k, all columns in interval are < k, so rectangle squares are in B for row k. So rectangle includes squares from both A and B. Does it include any uncovered squares? For any row r between i and k inclusive, the diagonal column is r. Since L > i and R < k, the interval (L,R) is a subset of (i, k). For any row r in [i,k], we have i < r < k. The interval may or may not contain r. Since L > i and R < k, it's possible that interval does not contain any integer r (if interval is small). For example, i=2, k=5, choose L=3,R=4. Then interval includes columns 3 and 4. For row 3, diagonal column is 3, which is included. So rectangle includes uncovered square (3,3). So not allowed. Choose i=2, k=5, L=4,R=4 (single column 4). Then interval includes column 4 only. For row 4, diagonal column is 4, which is included. So not allowed. Choose i=1, k=5, L=2,R=4. Then includes column 2,3,4. Row 2 diagonal column 2 is included. So not allowed. In general, for any interval (L,R) that lies between i and k, there will be some row r such that r = L (if L is integer between i+1 and k-1). Since rows are integer indices, there will be a row with index equal to L. That row's diagonal square is at column L, which is included. So any interval that includes any integer column will include a diagonal square for the row with that index. Since interval is integer endpoints, it includes at least one integer column. So rectangle will include uncovered square for that row. So crossing rectangle not allowed for identity permutation. But is it always the case for any permutation? Let's examine general permutation pi. For any rectangle that includes squares from both A and B, there exist rows i and k such that pi(i) < L <= R < pi(k). Since L,R are integer column indices. Then there exists a column c in [L,R] (c integer). Since L <= c <= R, and L > pi(i), c > pi(i). Also c < pi(k). Since pi is a permutation, the set of values {pi(1),...,pi(n)} is exactly {1,...,n}. So there exists a row r such that pi(r) = c. Because each column index appears exactly once as pi(i) for some i. Then row r has uncovered