n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:**  \n   Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   - *Upper region* (\\(i<j\\)): for each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     R_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     which is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  \n   - *Lower region* (\\(i>j\\)): for each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     C_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n     \\]\n     which is an \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) tile.  \n\n   **Verification:**  \n   *Every uncovered square* \\((i,i)\\) lies on the diagonal and is not contained in any \\(R_i\\) or \\(C_j\\) because each tile omits its own row or its own column.  \n   *Every other square* \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to exactly one horizontal tile \\(R_i\\) (its row is \\(i\\) and its column lies in \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \n   *Every square* \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) belongs to exactly one vertical tile \\(C_j\\) (its column is \\(j\\) and its row lies in \\(\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \n   The sets \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{C_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, so each unit square is covered at most once.  \n   The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement leaves exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the uncovered squares form a set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other squares must be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Tiles as rectangles\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle, i.e. a product \\(A\\times B\\) where  \n\\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a set of rows, \\(B\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a set of columns, and the rectangle consists of all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in A,\\;j\\in B\\).  \nBecause a tile may not contain a hole, we must have\n\\[\nA\\cap p^{-1}(B)=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the family of tiles gives a **partition** of the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal{E}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq p(i)\\}\n\\]\ninto disjoint products \\(A_k\\times B_k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound\n\nWithout loss of generality we may assume \\(p\\) is the identity permutation; any other permutation can be turned into the identity by renaming rows and columns, which does not change the number of tiles needed.\n\nHence the holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) and the set to be tiled is\n\\[\n\\mathcal{E}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i\\neq j\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) squares just **above** the diagonal:\n\\[\nU^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\\text{No rectangle }A\\times B\\text{ can contain two distinct squares of }U^{+}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nThen \\(A\\) must contain all rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,k\\) and \\(B\\) must contain all columns \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \nConsequently the diagonal square \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(A\\times B\\), contradicting the fact that diagonal squares are holes. ∎\n\nBecause the squares of \\(U^{+}\\) are pairwise distinct, the lemma implies that each rectangle covers **at most one** element of \\(U^{+}\\).  \nSince \\(|U^{+}|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the whole region above the diagonal.\n\nThe same argument applied to the \\(n-1\\) squares just **below** the diagonal,\n\\[\nU^{-}=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,