   Tile the two blocks with one tile each, the two strips with one tile each, and recurse on the two remaining \\(m\\times m\\) sub‑boards.  \n   This gives \\(f(2m+1)=4+2f(m)\\); solving with \\(f(1)=0\\) yields \\(f(2m+1)=2(2m+1)-2=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the construction match; therefore the minimum number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=\\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) (\\(n=2025\\)).  \nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile covers exactly the unit squares of a Cartesian product \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are non‑empty intervals of rows and columns, respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Reformulation  \n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered, and no tile may contain a point of \\(U\\).  \nThus we have to partition the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\}=([n]\\times[n])\\setminus U\n\\]\n\ninto axis‑aligned rectangles.  \nDenote by \\(f(n)\\) the minimum possible number of rectangles in such a partition (the answer we seek is \\(f(2025)\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   A universal lower bound  \n\nFor a given permutation \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\); all points of \\(S\\) belong to \\(C\\) (they are never holes).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle with \\(I,J\\) intervals, and assume that \\(R\\) contains no hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThen \\(R\\) contains **at most one** element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose that \\(R\\) contains two distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nThere are three possibilities.\n\n1. Both points are of the form \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\((j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   Since \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\), it also contains every row between them, in particular row \\(i+1\\).  \n   Its column interval contains the distinct columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j+1)\\); therefore it contains the whole interval between them, and hence the column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).  \n   Consequently \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) – a hole – lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n2. Both points are of the form \\((i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\((j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   By the same argument, \\(R\\) contains row \\(j\\) and column \\(\\sigma(j)\\), so the hole \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\) belongs to \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\n3. One point is \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and the other is \\((j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) (the two types).  \n   Assume \\(i\\le j\\).  \n   Then \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\), hence all rows from \\(i\\) up to \\(j+1\\); in particular it contains rows \\(i+1\\) and \\(j\\).  \n   Its column interval contains the distinct columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i+1)<\\sigma(j)\\), the leftmost column of \\(J\\) is \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\); together with row \\(i+1\\) this gives the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\in R\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i+1)>\\sigma(j)\\), the leftmost column of \\(J\\) is \\(\\sigma(j)\\); together with row \\(j\\) we obtain the hole \\((j,\\sigma(j))\\in R\\).  \n   In either sub‑case a hole lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption.\n\nThus