)\\in R\\).  \n   In either sub\u2011case a hole lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption.\n\nThus no rectangle avoiding all holes can contain two distinct points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the immediate lower bound\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge |S| = 2(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A construction attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge2\\), a placement of the holes and a tiling of the remaining squares using exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Even \\(n\\).**  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\).  \nDefine the permutation\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\ni+m & (1\\le i\\le m),\\\\[2pt]\ni-m & (m+1\\le i\\le 2m).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the first \\(m\\) rows have their holes in the last \\(m\\) columns, and the last \\(m\\) rows have their holes in the first \\(m\\) columns.\n\n*Step\u202f1.*  \nThe sub\u2011board consisting of rows \\(1,\\dots ,m\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,m\\) contains **no** hole; tile it with one rectangle \\(R_{1}\\).\n\n*Step\u202f2.*  \nSimilarly, rows \\(m+1,\\dots ,2m\\) together with columns \\(m+1,\\dots ,2m\\) is hole\u2011free; tile it with a second rectangle \\(R_{2}\\).\n\n*Step\u202f3.*  \nThe remaining squares form two disjoint \\(m\\times m\\) boards:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_{1}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m,\\;m+1\\le j\\le 2m\\},\\\\\nB_{2}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid m+1\\le i\\le 2m,\\;1\\le j\\le m\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\neach of which contains exactly one hole per row and per column (the restriction of \\(\\sigma\\) to the appropriate rows).  \nThus each of \\(B_{1},B_{2}\\) is a smaller instance of the original problem with size \\(m\\).\n\nApply the same construction recursively to \\(B_{1}\\) and \\(B_{2}\\).  \nIf \\(g(m)\\) denotes the number of rectangles used for a board of size \\(m\\), we obtain the recurrence\n\n\\[\ng(2m)=2+2\\,g(m),\\qquad g(1)=0. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSolving (3) by induction yields \\(g(2m)=2(2m)-2\\).  \nHence for even \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Odd \\(n\\).**  \nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\ni+m+1 & (1\\le i\\le m),\\\\[2pt]\nm+1   & (i=m+1),\\\\[2pt]\ni-(m+1) & (m+2\\le i\\le 2m+1).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus rows \\(1,\\dots ,m\\) have holes in the last \\(m\\) columns, rows \\(m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\) have holes in the first \\(m\\) columns, and the middle row \\(m+1\\) has its hole at column \\(m+1\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1.*  \nThe top\u2011left block \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) and the bottom\u2011right block \\(\\{m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\times\\{m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\) are hole\u2011free; tile each with one rectangle (two rectangles total).\n\n*Step\u202f2.*  \nThe middle row (row \\(m+1\\)) without its hole consists of the \\(m\\) squares \\((m+1,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq m+1\\); tile them with a single \\(1\\times m\\) rectangle.  \nSimilarly, the middle column (column \\(m+1\\)) without its hole consists of the \\(m\\) squares \\((i,m+1)\\) with \\(i\\neq m+1\\); tile them with another \\(1\\times m\\) rectangle.  \nThus we have added two more rectangles.\n\n*Step\u202f3.*  \nWhat remains are again two disjoint \\(m\\times m\\) boards (the top\u2011right and bottom\u2011left blocks), each containing a permutation of size \\(m\\).  \nTile each recursively.\n\nConsequently, letting \\(h(k)\\) be the number of rectangles for size \\(k\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nh(2m+1)=4+2\\,h(m),\\qquad h(1)=0. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInduction on \\(m\\) gives \\(h(2m+1)=2(2m+1)-2\\).  \nThus for odd \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Optimality  \n\nEquation\u202f(1) gives the universal lower bound \\(f(n)\\ge 2n-2\\).  \nEquations\u202f(4) and\u202f(7) provide constructions achieving exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles for every \\(n\\ge2\\).  \nHence the bound is tight and\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   The required number for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with 4048 rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer."
