i+m). So it's a permutation of size m. Similarly bottom-left block has holes at (i+m, i) for i=1..m. So indeed each subboard is of size m×m with a permutation pattern. So recursion works.\n\n- The recursion base case: f(1)=0. That's fine.\n\n- For odd case: They add a hole at (2m+1,2m+1). Then tile new row and column. The row rectangle of size 1×2m covers (2m+1, 1..2m), which does not contain any holes because holes in that row are only at column 2m+1. Similarly column rectangle covers (1..2m, 2m+1). So they are hole-free. So construction works.\n\nThus the solution appears correct and well-justified.\n\nPotential minor presentation issues: The solution uses some notation like \"hole\" to refer to uncovered squares. That's fine.\n\nPotential minor gaps: The proof of lower bound uses \"three cases possible\" and \"sub‑case\" but doesn't fully detail the case where i > j. However, they assume i ≤ j without loss of generality; that's acceptable. They also don't explicitly handle the case where rectangle contains points from S that are not adjacent in the sense of i and j being far apart, but the argument covers that.\n\nPotential minor gap: In the odd case, they say \"These two rectangles are disjoint and contain no holes because the extra row and column contain no holes except the common cell (2m+1,2m+1).\" Actually the extra row and column each contain the hole at (2m+1,2m+1) but the rectangles they tile exclude that cell. So they are disjoint and hole-free. This is correct, but they could have clarified that the row rectangle excludes column 2m+1 and column rectangle excludes row 2m+1. That's a minor justification gap.\n\nPotential minor gap: They didn't explicitly verify that the recursion yields integer number of rectangles for all n. But that's fine.\n\nThus at Level 3, we have only justification gaps (minor missing details) but no critical errors. According to the hierarchical decision tree, if only justification gaps, the verdict is PASS.\n\nThus final verdict: