lly above holes in some columns and wholly below holes in other columns, but not containing any holes? Let's examine: Suppose rectangle's row interval is [a,b]. For each column j in its column range, either all cells in that column within the rectangle are above the hole (i.e., b < r_j) or all are below (i.e., a > r_j). If the rectangle's row interval is entirely above the hole for each column, then b < r_j for all j => b < min_j r_j => rectangle is in U^+. If rectangle's row interval is entirely below the hole for each column, then a > r_j for all j => a > max_j r_j => rectangle is in U^-. But could there be a rectangle where for some columns b < r_j (above) and for others a > r_j (below) but the rectangle's row interval does not intersect any hole? That would require that for each column j, either b < r_j or a > r_j. But if the rectangle's row interval is [a,b] and there exist columns where b < r_j and columns where a > r_j, then we must have a > max_{j where a > r_j} r_j and b < min_{j where b < r_j} r_j. For the rectangle to exist, we need a <= b. So we need max_{j in set A} r_j < min_{j in set B} r_j, where set A = columns where a > r_j (i.e., rectangle below holes) and set B = columns where b < r_j (rectangle above holes). If there is a gap between the holes of those columns, it's possible that the rectangle sits in that gap. But does that rectangle intersect any hole? No, because for each column in set A, the rectangle is entirely below the hole; for each column in set B, it's entirely above the hole. However, the rectangle includes columns from both sets, so the row interval [a,b] must be simultaneously above holes in set B and below holes in set A. This is possible only if the holes in set A are all below the holes in set B, i.e., max_{A} r_j < min_{B} r_j. But is that possible given that the holes are a permutation? Yes, it's possible to have some columns with holes in low rows and some columns with holes in high rows, leaving a gap in the middle. For exam