\).  \nIf the row interval \\([a,b]\\) and the column interval \\([c,d]\\) intersect, i.e. there exists \\(k\\) with \\(a\\le k\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le k\\le d\\), then the square \\((k,k)\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption. Hence the two intervals are disjoint.  \n\nThere are two possibilities:\n\n* **Above the diagonal:** \\(b<c\\). Then every row index in \\(R\\) is smaller than every column index, so all squares of \\(R\\) satisfy \\(r<c\\le c'\\); consequently \\(r<c'\\) and \\(R\\) lies strictly above the main diagonal.\n\n* **Below the diagonal:** \\(d<a\\). Then every column index is smaller than every row index, so all squares satisfy \\(c'<r\\); thus \\(R\\) lies strictly below the diagonal.\n\nNo rectangle can contain squares from both sides of the diagonal. Therefore the set of off‑diagonal squares splits into two independent parts:\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly above the diagonal)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly below the diagonal)}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery tile belongs either to \\(U\\) (type “above’’) or to \\(L\\) (type “below’’).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) cells of \\(U\\) that are immediately above the diagonal:\n\\[\n\\alpha_i=(i,i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle of type “above’’ that contains \\(\\alpha_i\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) contains row \\(i\\) and column \\(i+1\\), its row interval satisfies \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and its column interval satisfies \\(c\\le i+1\\le d\\).  \nBecause a rectangle of type “above’’ must obey \\(b<c\\), we have\n\\[\nb<c\\le i+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad i\\le b.\n\\]\nThus \\(b=i\\) and consequently \\(c=i+1\\). Hence any rectangle covering \\(\\alpha_i\\) has maximal row index exactly \\(i\\) and minimal column index exactly \\(i+1\\).\n\nIf a single rectangle covered two distinct cells \\(\\alpha_i\\) and \\(\\alpha_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), it would have to satisfy \\(b=i\\) and also \\(b=j\\), impossible. Therefore **each rectangle of type “above’’ can contain at most one cell \\(\\alpha_i\\)**.\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\alpha_i\\) must all be covered, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles of type “above’’ are required.\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the \\(n-1\\) cells immediately below the diagonal,\n\\[\n\\beta_i=(i+1,i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nshows that each rectangle of type “below’’ can contain at most one \\(\\beta_i\\). Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles of type “below’’ are needed.\n\nSumming the two sides, any tiling that satisfies (1) uses **at least**\n\\[\n2(n-1)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the lower bound\n\nDefine, for each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\), the rectangle\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(r,c):1\\le r\\le i,\\; i+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nHere the row interval is \\([1,i]\\) and the column interval is \\([i+1,n]\\); clearly \\(i<b<c=i+1\\), so \\(R_i\\) lies above the diagonal and avoids all diagonal squares.\n\nSimilarly, for each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\), define\n\\[\nS_i=\\{(r,c):i\\le r\\le n,\\; 1\\le c\\le i-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nNow the column interval \\([1,i-1]\\) lies entirely to the left of the row interval \\([i,n]\\); thus \\(S_i\\) lies below the diagonal and contains no diagonal cell.\n\n**Coverage of \\(U\\).**  \nTake any cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\). Set \\(i=r\\). Then \\(1\\le r\\le i\\) and \\(i+1\\le c\\le n\\), so \\((r,c)\\in R_i\\). Moreover, if \\(i\\neq r\\) the inequalities fail, so \\((r,c)\\) belongs to exactly one rectangle \\(R_r\\). Hence \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(U\\).\n\n**Coverage of \\(L\\).**  \nTake any cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\). Set \\(i=r\\). Then \\(i\\le r\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le i-1\\), so \\((r,c)\\in S_i\\). Uniqueness follows as before. Thus \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) partitions \\(L\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) are disjoint, and together they cover every off‑diagonal unit square exactly once. The total number of rectangles used is\n\\[\n| \\{R_i\\}|+| \\{S_i\\}| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nEquation (5) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles for any admissible placement, while equation (8) exhibits a placement that uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles. Hence the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nFor the original board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid, while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
