s for two different rows (otherwise the interval would have to be simultaneously \\(\\le\\) one hole and \\(\\ge\\) another).  Hence a tile is **entirely** contained either in  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le\\ell_i\\}\\quad\\text{(the left side of all holes)},\n\\]\nor in  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(the right side of all holes)}.\n\\]\n\nThus the whole tiling is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left region \\(L\\)\n\nThe numbers \\(\\ell_i\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular each value \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) occurs exactly once.  \nFix a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) and consider the square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\), the rightmost square of the left segment of that row.  \nLet \\(T\\) be the (unique) tile of the tiling of \\(L\\) that contains \\((i,\\ell_i)\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\subseteq L\\), its column interval \\([c,d]\\) satisfies \\(d\\le\\ell_i\\).  Since \\((i,\\ell_i)\\in T\\), we must have \\(d\\ge\\ell_i\\); therefore \\(d=\\ell_i\\).  Hence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nIf another row \\(i'\\) also belongs to \\(T\\), then its left segment must contain column \\(\\ell_i\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{i'}\\ge\\ell_i\\).  Thus \\(\\ell_i\\) is the **minimum** of the set \\(\\{\\ell_{i'}\\mid i'\\text{ belongs to }T\\}\\).\n\nConsequently distinct positive values of \\(\\ell_i\\) give rise to distinct tiles: the tile whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\) is uniquely determined by the row whose left segment has length \\(k\\).  Since the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) has \\(n-1\\) elements, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nThe right lengths \\(r_i=n-\\pi(i)\\) also form 