            "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. By permuting rows and columns we may assume that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\).  \n2. Prove that any admissible rectangle (a tile) cannot contain a diagonal square; consequently each rectangle lies entirely **above** the diagonal (all its rows are strictly smaller than all its columns) or entirely **below** the diagonal (all its columns are strictly smaller than all its rows).  \n3. Show that in the upper‑triangular part \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\) the cells \\((i,i+1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) must belong to distinct rectangles. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells above the diagonal.  \n4. By symmetry the same argument applied to the lower‑triangular part \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\) yields another \\(n-1\\) necessary rectangles.  \n5. Conclude that any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n6. Exhibit a construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles: for each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal tile covering the squares to the right of the diagonal \\(\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (if \\(i<n\\)) and a horizontal tile covering the squares to the left of the diagonal \\(\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,i-1\\}\\) (if \\(i>1\\)). These \\(2n-2\\) tiles are disjoint, cover all non‑diagonal squares, and leave precisely one uncovered square in each row and column.  \n7. For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles, which is minimal.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square is identified by its row and column indices \\((r,c)\\) with \\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to diagonal holes\nPermuting rows and columns does not change the number of tiles needed, because a permutation of the grid merely relabels the rows and columns.  \nHence we may assume that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) (and also in column \\(i\\)) is the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\) for every \\(i\\).  \nAll other squares must be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of a permissible rectangle\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore the set of rows it occupies is a contiguous interval \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns it occupies is a contiguous interval \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nWrite the rectangle as \\(R=I\\times J\\).\n\nIf a rectangle contained a diagonal square \\((k,k)\\), then \\(k\\in I\\) and \\(k\\in J\\).  \nSince the diagonal squares are uncovered, any admissible rectangle must avoid all points \\((k,k)\\).  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) contains no diagonal square, then either  \n\n\\[\n\\max I<\\min J\\qquad\\text{(all its squares satisfy row < column, i.e. \\(R\\) lies above the diagonal)},\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\n\\max J<\\min I\\qquad\\text{(all its squares satisfy row > column, i.e. \\(R\\) lies below the diagonal)}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two squares \\((i,j)\\) and \\((i',j')\\) with \\(i<j\\) and \\(i'>j'\\).  \nThen \\(i\\in I,\\;j\\in J,\\;i'\\in I,\\;j'\\in J\\).  \nBecause \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals, we have \\(\\max I\\ge i'\\) and \\(\\min J\\le j\\).  \nIf both types of squares occur, we would have \\(\\max I\\ge i'>j'\\ge\\min J\\), contradicting the requirement that a rectangle cannot contain a diagonal square (otherwise \\(\\max I\\ge\\min J\\) would force some \\(k\\) with \\(k\\in I\\cap J\\), giving the diagonal square \\((k,k)\\)).  \nThus all squares of \\(R\\) satisfy the same inequality, which yields the two possibilities above. ∎\n\nConsequently the board splits into two independent regions:\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j):1\\le i<j\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly upper‑triangular)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly lower‑triangular)}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile lies entirely in \\(U\\) or entirely in \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A lower bound for the upper‑triangular region\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) cells\n\\[\na_i=(i,i+1)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nNo admissible rectangle can contain two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_{i'}\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(\\max I<\\min J\\) by Lemma 1) contains \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_{i'}\\) where \\(i<i'\\).  \nFrom \\(a_i\\in R\\) we obtain \\(i\\in I\\) and \\(i+1\\in J\\).  \nFrom \\(a_{i'}\\in R\\) we obtain \\(i'\\in I\\) and \\(i'+1\\in J\\).  \nSince \\(I\\) is an interval, \\(\\max I\\ge i'\\); since \\(J\\) is an interval, \\(\\min J\\le i+1\\).  \nThe condition \\(\\max I<\\min J\\) therefore yields \\(i'<i+1\\), i.e. \\(i'\\le i\\), contradicting \\(i<i'\\). ∎\n\nThus each cell \\(a_i\\) must belong to a distinct rectangle.  \nHence any partition of \\(U\\) into admissible rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. A lower bound for the lower‑triangular region\n\nBy symmetry, apply Lemma 2 to the cells\n\\[\nb_i=(i,i-1)\\qquad(i=2,3,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\nExactly the same argument (now using the case \\(\\max J<\\min I\\) of Lemma 1) shows that no rectangle can contain two distinct \\(b_i\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile lies either in \\(U\\) or in \\(L\\); the two families are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling of the whole board must contain at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) define two horizontal rectangles:\n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{(right of the diagonal, empty if }i=n\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\}\\qquad\\text{(left of the diagonal, empty if }i=1\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nAll these rectangles are axis‑parallel, their interiors are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non‑diagonal square exactly once.  \nThe only uncovered squares are the diagonal ones \\((i,i)\\), one per row and one per column, as required.\n\nThe number of non‑empty rectangles is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right strips }R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}}\n+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left strips }L_2,\\dots ,L_n}\n=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nTaking \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
