### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, and each column must also contain exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBy permuting the columns according to \\(p^{-1}\\) we may assume\n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Tiles as rank‑1 binary matrices  \n\nA rectangular tile occupies a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); it covers exactly the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}.\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the binary vectors  \n\n\\[\nu^{(R)}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n},\\qquad u^{(R)}_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&i\\in R,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&i\\notin R,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad \nv^{(C)}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n},\\qquad v^{(C)}_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&j\\in C,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&j\\notin C.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe indicator matrix of the tile is the outer product  \n\n\\[\nM(R,C)=u^{(R)}\\,(v^{(C)})^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\na binary matrix of rank 1.  \nBecause a tile must not cover any uncovered square, we have  \n\n\\[\nu^{(R)}_i\\,v^{(C)}_i=0\\qquad\\text{for every }i,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the diagonal.  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) put a tile  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe tile \\(R_i\\times C_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole \\(i\\)-th row except the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\