{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) is a permutation.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le N,\\;j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le N,\\;j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus every covered unit square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\), and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A tile cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\)\n\nAssume a rectangular tile \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\in L\\) and a cell \\((i,k)\\in R\\) (the two cells may be in the same row or in different rows).  \nIf they are in the same row, then the column interval of \\(T\\) contains the column \\(p(i)\\); consequently \\((i,p(i))\\) lies in \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that uncovered squares are not covered.  \nIf they are in different rows, say \\((i,j)\\in L\\) with \\(j<p(i)\\) and \\((k,\\ell)\\in R\\) with \\(\\ell>p(k)\\), then the column interval of \\(T\\) contains both \\(p(i)\\) and \\(p(k)\\).  \nSince the row interval of \\(T\\) contains the rows \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), at least one of the uncovered squares \\((i,p(i))\\) or \\((k,p(k))\\) belongs to \\(T\\), again a contradiction.  \nHence every tile is contained wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of tiles covering \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the *left‑neighbor cell*\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\in L .\n\\]\n\nBecause at most one row can satisfy \\(p(i)=1\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(|\\mathcal L|\\ge N-1\\).\n\n**Claim.** No tile can contain two distinct cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(\\ell_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\ell_k=(k,p(k)-1)\\) be two cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nIf \\(p(i)<p(k)\\) then the column interval of any rectangle containing both cells contains the column \\(p(i)\\); consequently the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) lies inside the rectangle, which is impossible.  \nIf \\(p(i)>p(k)\\) the same argument applied to column \\(p(k)\\) shows that \\((k,p(k))\\) would be inside the rectangle.  \nThus the claim holds. ∎\n\nSince each tile can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal L\\), covering all cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\) requires at least \\(|\\mathcal L|\\ge N-1\\) tiles.  \nTherefore any tiling of the whole board uses at least \\(N-1\\) tiles that lie in \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles covering \\(R\\)\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<N\\) define the *right‑neighbor cell*\n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\in R .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{r_i\\mid p(i)<N\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(|\\mathcal R|\\ge N-1\\).  \nExactly as in the previous claim, no rectangle can contain two distinct cells of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(N-1\\) tiles are required to cover all of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  No tile can serve both regions\n\nFrom Section 1 every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).  \nHence the tiles that cover the cells of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are disjoint from those that cover the cells of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nThus the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \nThen\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles for \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le N-1\\) take the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{L}= \\{(r,c)\\mid c+1\\le r\\le N\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has height \\(N-c\\) and width \\(1\\).  \nThese \\(N-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*Tiles for \\(R\\).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le N-1\\) take the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_r^{R}= \\{(r,c)\\mid r+1\\le c\\le N\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has height \\(1\\) and width \\(N-r\\).  \nThese \\(N-1\\) rectangles are disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThe \\(2(N-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{L}\\}_{c=1}^{N-1}\\cup\\{T_r^{R}\\}_{r=1}^{N-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, lie on grid lines, and cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), exactly one per row and one per column.  \n\nThus a tiling with \\(2N-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2N-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2N-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2N-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nReview the “Summary” and “Detailed Solution” sections to ensure:\n\n* All mathematical statements are expressed in TeX.  \n* The lower‑bound argument correctly uses the left‑neighbor and right‑neighbor cells and shows that each tile can contain at most one such cell, and that tiles for the two regions are disjoint.  \n* The construction for the diagonal permutation indeed uses exactly \\(2(N-1)\\) rectangles and covers all required squares.  \n* No step relies on unproven intuition; every claim is justified by a clear logical argument.  \n* The final answer is presented in the required \\(\\boxed{\\,}\\) format."
