\\) we may assume  \n   \\(p(i)=i\\) for every \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal.\n   This transformation does not change the number of tiles.\n\n2. **Critical cells.**  \n   For the diagonal configuration define  \n\n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le N\\},\\qquad \n   R=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le N-1\\},\n   \\]\n   and let \\(S=L\\cup R\\).  \n   Then \\(|S|=2(N-1)\\).\n\n3. **Lemma (incompatibility).**  \n   *Lemma.* No axis‑aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines can contain two\n   distinct cells of \\(S\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let a rectangle \\(T\\) contain two cells of \\(S\\).  \n   Write \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) where \\([a,b]\\) (resp. \\([c,d]\\)) is the interval\n   of its rows (resp. columns).  \n\n   *Both cells in \\(R\\).*  \n   Suppose \\(T\\) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   Then \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1<j+1\\le d\\); hence the column interval\n   \\([c,d]\\) contains the column \\(j\\).  Because row \\(j\\) belongs to \\([a,b]\\),\n   the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\) lies in \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that diagonal\n   cells are uncovered.\n\n   *Both cells in \\(L\\).*  \n   The argument is symmetric: if \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((j,j-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belong\n   to \\(T\\), then \\([c,d]\\) contains the column \\(i\\) and \\([a,b]\\) contains the\n   row \\(i\\), so \\((i,i)\\) is inside \\(T\\), a contradiction.\n\n   *One cell in \\(L\\) and one in \\(R\\).*  \n   Assume \\(T\\) contains \\((i,i-1)\\in L\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\in R\\) with \\(i\\le j\\).\n   If \\(i=j\\) then \\(T\\) contains both \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((i,i+1)\\); the column\n   interval then contains the column \\(i\\) and the row interval contains the row\n   \\(i\\), so \\((i,i)\\in T\\), contradiction.  \n   If \\(i<j\\) then \\(c\\le i-1<j+1\\le d\\) and \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\); consequently the\n   column interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the column \\(j\\) and the row interval\n   contains the row \\(j\\), whence \\((j,j)\\in T\\), again a contradiction.  \n   The case \\(j<i\\) is symmetric.  ∎\n\n   Because a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\), at least\n   \\(|S|=2(N-1)\\) rectangles are necessary.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Keep the diagonal uncovered.  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le N-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     V_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid c+1\\le r\\le N\\},\n     \\]\n\n     which has height \\(N-c\\) and width \\(1\\).  \n   - For each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le N-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     H_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid r+1\\le c\\le N\\},\n     \\]\n\n     which has height \\(1\\) and width \\(N-r\\).\n\n   The \\(V_c\\) cover exactly the cells below the diagonal, the \\(H_r\\) cover exactly\n   the cells above the diagonal, and the two families are disjoint.  \n   Hence the \\(2(N-1)\\) rectangles cover every unit square except the diagonal\n   squares \\((i,i)\\), i.e. exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\n5. **Minimality.**  \n   The lower bound of \\(2(N-1)\\) tiles and the explicit construction using exactly\n   \\(2(N-1)\\) tiles coincide.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2(N-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**